WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.425 --> 00:00:01.620
- Welcome everyone.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.050
Today I'm standing in front of
my old university in Munich.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:07.840
Here I completed my undergraduate studies.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.180
So old memories for
sure are coming up here.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.640
And the video of today

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.570
is about time management.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.600
I am sharing with you my insights

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.500
on how to manage your time right.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.620
How to get your time management
the way it should be.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.050
And indeed, there's lots
of information out there

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.520
available on the topic.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.490
There's the Pomodoro technique,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.160
calendar blocking, getting things done,

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.770
lots of project management tips like this.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:36.300
And I believe that actually
most of them do not work.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.470
Most of these things are not effective.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.000
Most of these things will not get you

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.110
where you wanna be at.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.420
So today I'm sharing exactly these things

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.530
because I tried many of these things out.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.570
And indeed my personal insight is that,

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.200
even though you might spend
some time trying it out,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.980
in the end they will not deliver you

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.910
the results that you need.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.080
My name is Heinrich

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.100
and welcome on my channel, "Firm Learning"

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.680
where I want to help you

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.190
to become successful in the
first years of your career.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.820
And the reason why I'm here
at University of Munich,

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.950
when I'm talking about time management,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.730
is that here it is exactly

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.150
where I personally got my
first insights on the topic.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.330
And indeed, when I started
in my undergraduate,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.640
I studied for two
independent bachelor programs

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.920
at the same time,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.680
bachelor in philosophy and
the bachelor in business.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.910
I completed both within
the regular study period.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.060
Of course I was also part

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.220
of different extracurricular activities.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.660
I spent a semester abroad

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.670
at the University of
Milan, Bocconi University.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.140
So one could argue that,

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.490
maybe I'm not too bad at time management.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.370
But what did I do?

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.880
What crazy techniques that did I use?

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.990
And the reason is, the
truth is, I didn't use any

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.700
of these special time
management techniques.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.720
And this is one of the later learned

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.550
the exact same thing that is true

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.500
for working in consulting.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.330
During my time at McKinsey,

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.070
of course, most consultants
were pretty productive.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.810
If you just look at the
output of these people

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.240
add the things that these people get done.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.660
Yes. One could argue that they must have

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.670
crazy time management
techniques some place

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:09.987
to get all this done.

00:02:09.987 --> 00:02:13.380
And of course they will use
basic project management

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.410
best practices, such as
having, to do lists, checklist,

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.470
project management techniques like that,

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.500
but they are not using any Pomodoro,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.750
calendar blocking techniques like that.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.460
This is just not what is happening.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.730
So what is the common
thing that both I did

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.330
at the university of Munich,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.264
and then later all the people do

00:02:33.264 --> 00:02:36.140
that are working in consulting.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.150
What are they doing right

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.380
to manage their time when it's not

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.130
all these crazy techniques that
everybody is talking about?

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.870
So the one key factor that really keeps

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.650
consultants productive, is just pressure,

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.820
external pressure, external commitments.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.130
This is just the truth.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.750
This is what it's all about.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:54.583
And indeed many people

00:02:54.583 --> 00:02:56.780
struggle with procrastination.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.560
And for sure, I might suffer from that

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.350
in my private life as well.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.050
But why is it the case,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.270
that if you work in consulting

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.730
or in any other high paced career,

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.700
why is it the case that
in these environments

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.330
nobody is suffering from procrastination,

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:12.170
I can tell you.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.480
And the main reason is,

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.230
that just people need to deliver.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:18.241
Every single day, there is
an important client meeting,

00:03:18.241 --> 00:03:21.190
an important internal
problem solving session.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.380
The next vehicle that you need to prepare

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.190
you are just under
pressure for the whole day.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.780
This is what keeps you productive.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.280
And indeed there is a law that

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.010
probably some of you also know

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.164
it's called Parkinson's law.

00:03:34.164 --> 00:03:37.580
And this law says that the
work that you will need to do

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.370
will always take up all the time

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.170
that you allocate to that work.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.420
So if you need to achieve
and complete something

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.410
if you have an hour for
it, you will, in most cases

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:48.600
get it done in an hour.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.090
If you have a day for it, you will easily

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.060
be able to fill it up for a day,

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.170
because then maybe you do some breaks.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.630
Maybe you do some other things.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.520
You Polish it a little bit,
make it look a little bit nice

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.050
or whatever the task,
whatever the activity is.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.920
But in the end, the result
will be more or less the same

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.330
it just took you much,
much longer to complete it.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.740
And I had the very same
experience when I studied here

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:13.130
at university of Munich

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:15.770
for my undergraduate
programs, what kept me

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.930
productive were not crazy
time management techniques.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:19.990
It was just the pressure

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.410
of needing to complete
all these different things

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.800
in a short amount of time.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.610
Same is true in consulting.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.200
It's not about time management techniques

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.370
but just the deadlines and the
pressure that you are facing.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.770
And indeed, another
concept that really plays

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.390
into this is the concept of adaptation.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.730
So I remember when I worked in consulting

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.670
of course everybody
was working long hours.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.640
You are often sitting after midnight

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.540
just leaving lots of work
that needed to be done.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:47.960
Then I did the MBA.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.080
I had a break for about one year.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.760
And of course, then
the whole life changed.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.230
We had lots of time with all
the students during the MBA.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:59.830
And suddenly I remember days
where I had three lectures

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.337
on one day, how tired I felt.

00:05:02.337 --> 00:05:05.830
I felt this was the
most exhausting day ever

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.500
only because I was sitting in
three lectures on a given day.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.150
And again, I'll remember
when then after the MBA

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.400
I was back in consulting and I
was on my very first project.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.500
And again, the first days,
the first one or two weeks

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.330
it just was so difficult.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.005
I felt so tired after this time.

00:05:24.005 --> 00:05:28.070
I remember sitting at home
and I thought to myself

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:28.903
I cannot do it.

00:05:28.903 --> 00:05:29.810
It's just too much.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.340
I cannot handle this anymore.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.210
This is just not the job. I asked myself

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.090
how was I able to deal with this, to bear

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.380
with this in the past,
but then after one or two

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.290
or three weeks, again,
you went back to normal.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.020
You just got used to it again.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.100
So here are the key
messages you get used to it.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.070
If you feel tired, if you
feel you cannot do more

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:51.440
you have too much pressure.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.960
It's always relative to your
level of tasks and activities.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.290
If you're just used to do more

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:58.350
you will also able to do more.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.420
So this is just the key
insight that you need to

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:03.233
understand your ability
to get more work done

00:06:03.233 --> 00:06:06.100
will not depend on any
time management techniques

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.270
but just the number of
things, the amount of work

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:12.930
that you have on your plate
and that you need to complete

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.010
not tomorrow

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:17.960
not a week from now a month
from now, but right now.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.030
So having said that you might say Heinrich

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:21.170
well that makes sense.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:22.770
Hopefully you agree with that

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.890
but how can we make this practical?

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.420
This is pretty theoretical
after all, Isn't it?

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.710
So let me share with you three
tangible tips, three pieces

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:33.550
of advice on how you can
implement this in your daily life.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.620
First is compatibility of tasks.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.960
So try to pick different activities

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.940
different tasks that are
complimentary to each other

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.800
and do not necessarily conflict.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.190
So when I was in university

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.640
and studied philosophy
and business, in business

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.110
it was the case that at
the end of every semester

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.260
there were lots of exams I needed to take.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.940
In philosophy on the other
hand, during the semester

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.650
there were term papers,
coursework assignment

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:57.920
that I needed to complete.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:06:59.070
So what this meant is that

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.540
during the semester I needed
to focus on philosophy

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.110
getting these term papers out.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:04.910
And then at the end of the semester

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:06.600
I was able to focus on business,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.920
on the business exams because
there hardly were any exams

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:11.470
in my philosophy undergrad at all.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.450
So while I was able to do all these things

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:16.910
in parallel time-wise they
did not really collide.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.830
They were complimentary to each other.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:20.320
And this is also important for you.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.210
If you look at all the things
that you might be interested

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:27.130
in doing, can you pick things
that really go well together?

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:28.720
If you're able to pick activities

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.600
which are not in conflict to each other

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.610
but can really leverage your
dead time in the evenings

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.830
or on the weekends, this
is what we're really

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.810
make the difference for
you in the long-term.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.330
My second advice is to
deliberately put yourself

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.630
in situations where you're
creating external commitments.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:45.570
And here, again, sharing a personal story.

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:46.670
So several years ago

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:49.063
I was not really a sportive person at all.

00:07:49.063 --> 00:07:51.440
I was rarely doing any sport

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.560
and while today I'm still
not the fittest guy.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.680
At least I managed to pick
up a regular running habit.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:57.540
And in the last year

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.310
it's usually that I managed
to finish two marathons.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.050
And for me there was one thing that really

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:02.930
made the difference

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:05.090
in picking up this regular training habit.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:07.980
And this was signing up to the marathon

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.360
or before that signing
up to the half marathon.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:12.620
Once I had this date locked

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.190
well I knew that I would
go there, take the run.

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:18.380
And also some friends joining
me, suddenly I had a reason.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:19.530
I had a higher purpose

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.280
of why I was training, why I was running.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:23.290
And just by that alone

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:26.130
I was able to then exercise
several days a week

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.510
just improve my form to
get ready for that event.

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:29.343
So now every year

00:08:29.343 --> 00:08:32.070
I try to register myself for
one of these running events

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.710
just to have a reason to
have a goal, to run towards.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:35.680
You know, translate that

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.140
to your areas where you're
wanting to be successful.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.830
What can you do to
create these commitments

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.060
to create this artificial
external pressure

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:44.440
to hold you accountable?

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:46.520
And the little things that
you can do are to talk

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:48.250
about these things, to other people

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.640
to maybe even find an
accountability partner

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.980
with whom you regularly talk

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:53.950
about these things and
make your commitments.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:56.560
Don't just keep it in
head, but make it public

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.200
create external commitments
to keep your cultivated.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.810
And my third and maybe most
important tip that at least

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:04.220
for me personally,
really worked well is to

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:07.080
try to always to commit
to more things always try

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.390
to get more things done than you believe

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:11.310
you're realistically really able to do.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.260
And if you do that, then yes,
maybe some things will fail.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:16.260
Maybe you will not be
able to achieve every

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:19.500
little thing that you want to,
but then if you aim for 200%

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.390
at least to get 150% done.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:23.800
On the other hand from the
beginning, you're only aiming

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.370
for the a hundred percent
due to Parkinson's law,

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.530
you're likely still
will not even be able to

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.040
achieve this a hundred percent.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:33.150
So deliberately overload
yourself with goals,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.730
with activities be okay to maybe fail

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.940
with some of these things.
So accept that job.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:40.580
Sign up for that sports
training group take on

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.940
this extra voluntary leadership commitment

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:44.670
in your community, commit to work

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.160
on your own personal business project

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.000
in the evenings or on the weekend.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:52.003
And yes, if you do all that,
maybe not everything will

00:09:52.003 --> 00:09:54.980
work the way you hope to,
but I would be very surprised

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.670
if you do others, it would
not get done much more

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.530
than if you only take it slow

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:01.440
and not do all these
things from the beginning.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.270
So hope that there were
some interesting points

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.050
that you took away from this as always

